# Portable USB: mm.usb, mm.usb.device, and mm.usb.host

# Status and authority

This document specifies the portable USB architecture, interfaces, platform
providers, and class modules for release v1.3.1. It describes the design and
contracts of `mm.usb` (shared vocabulary), `mm.usb.device` (device stack
interface), and `mm.usb.host` (host stack interface).

docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy remains authoritative for mm.mcu's MCU transports
and block storage. docs/modules-stdio.mdy remains authoritative for mm.stdio's
console contract. docs/modules-platforms.mdy remains authoritative for the
platform-interface and platform-provider resolution mechanism.

The interfaces change no existing interface. mm.stdio, mm.mcu, mm.display, and
mm.sketch keep working exactly as they do.

# Architectural motivation

USB fits neither existing interface:
- mm.mcu's transports are byte streams (UART) and synchronous register buses
  (I2C, SPI); USB is packetised, enumerated, endpoint-addressed, and driven
  by standard control requests and descriptors.
- mm.stdio is a console and nothing more.
- mm.display demonstrates the architecture that succeeds here: a focused,
  allocation-free, project-owned platform interface owning lifecycle and
  transfer, with policy above it (USB class modules) and hardware below it
  (a UDC, a host controller, or a vendor stack).

Two interfaces are defined rather than one because the two sides share only
vocabulary:
- A device answers one host's requests on its own endpoints.
- A host enumerates many devices and addresses their endpoints.
A program is usually one or the other, and a target board provides one, the other,
or both.

# Layering

Opening fence
application or class module (CDC, vendor protocol, HID, MSC device)
             |                                   |
       mm.usb.device                        mm.usb.host
             |                                   |
  device provider                        host provider
  (platform.pico.usb.device,             (platform.pico.usb.host,
   platform.linux.usb.device,             platform.linux.usb.host,
   test stand-in)                         test stand-in)
             |                                   |
  TinyUSB device stack /                 TinyUSB host stack /
  Linux FunctionFS                       libusb over Linux usbfs
             \_____________ mm.usb ______________/
                    (shared types and status)
Closing fence

mm.usb is an ordinary module of shared vocabulary. mm.usb.device and
mm.usb.host are each a platform-interface, so a platform provider is linked only
when an executable's closure reaches the interface it serves.

